SHOOTING DISCIPLINE (avoid the "dead zone" — a frequent failure that makes a
team log ZERO shots for a whole match): it happens when the pass-first rule
releases the ball as soon as the carrier reaches the final third while the shoot
rule only fires from very close range, leaving a band where the carrier always
passes and never shoots. Prevent it: when YOU carry the ball in an attacking
position (FWD, or an advanced MID/DEF) roughly within the top of the opponent's
penalty area (about a fifth of the field from their goal line, read from field
geometry — never hardcoded), reasonably central with a sight of goal and no
defender right on top, return Action::Shoot instead of recycling. Make the shoot
condition OVERLAP the pass-first condition for advanced central carriers — do
not let it sit strictly closer to goal. IMPORTANT: this is the CARRIER deciding
to shoot, NOT a reason to push extra players forward — do not over-commit the
team and expose yourself on the counter.

OFFSIDE (IFAB Law 11, judged on the x-axis at the moment a team-mate PASSES):
a team-mate is in an offside position if they are strictly inside the
opponents' half AND strictly nearer the opponents' goal line than both the
ball and the second-last opponent (any role — usually GK + last defender).
Level with the second-last opponent = onside. If a player who was in an
offside position when the pass was struck is the FIRST to control that pass,
play stops: free kick to the opposing team at that spot (possession lost).
No offside from goal kicks, throw-ins, or corner kicks.
Tactics: attackers — time runs to stay level with the last defender until
the pass is struck, then sprint. Passers — check the receiver is onside
BEFORE passing forward. Defenders — hold a coordinated high line to trap
goal-hangers.

